.lua
book.chapter(34, "Capabilities and runtime architecture")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Installation and authority are independent"
.pp
A host module installer adds declarations and primitive implementations.
The primitive allowlist decides which non-core operations may be invoked.
It accepts exact names, module patterns, and a global wildcard. Trusted
register_primitive grants the registered name, while install_primitive is
designed for libraries that require a later permission decision. This
separation lets an application compose available libraries without granting
every capability merely because a header was included.
.pp
Design permissions around the task a script performs. A formatting rule may
need only a pure string helper, while a build script may deliberately need
source loading. Granting an entire module is convenient but broader than
granting one operation. Record the policy alongside the application version
so a script failure after an upgrade can be diagnosed as a capability change
rather than mistaken for a parser or type-system regression.
.lua
book.algorithm("Construct a task-specific runtime", {
    "Create a fresh Session owned by one execution context.",
    "Install the host modules required by the task.",
    "Grant exact primitive names or intentional module patterns.",
    "Load a known standard-library tree and check diagnostics.",
    "Configure output routing and recursion limits.",
    "Execute requests through the owner and retain structured results."
})
.endlua
.sh 2 "An allowlist is not process isolation"
.pp
Core IO, source imports, native allocation, and unbounded computation remain
relevant even when the non-core allowlist is empty. Frontend.eval creates
another language session but can still execute core effects. A recursion
limit does not stop an infinite tail-recursive loop, and a small expression
can request substantial memory. These observations describe concrete runtime
boundaries; they are not reasons to confuse ordinary script errors with
operating-system enforcement.
.pp
When executing hostile programs, place the interpreter in a separate process
with appropriate time, memory, filesystem, and communication restrictions.
The parent process can terminate a worker that exceeds its budget without
requiring a cooperative callback from every evaluator operation. Define a
protocol that separates source, configuration, returned values, output, and
diagnostics. A worker restart also provides a clear way to discard partially
mutated language state after a failed request.
.sh 2 "Choose a session ownership model"
.pp
One owner per session is the simplest concurrency contract. A desktop
application can send requests to a dedicated worker; a batch service can
create a session per job; a trusted interactive workbench can retain one
session for the user's entire conversation. These models differ in startup
cost, retained definitions, and recovery semantics. Choose intentionally
rather than sharing one mutable session because it happens to be globally
accessible from host code.
.lua
book.uml({
    {id="H", name="Host coordinator", members={"request queue", "result publication"}},
    {id="W", name="Worker owner", members={"one Session", "explicit library policy"}},
    {id="R", name="Resources", members={"buffers; tables", "output sink"}}
}, {{"H", "W", "submits"}, {"W", "R", "uses"}},
    "Session ownership separates coordination from evaluation")
.endlua
.pp
Handle-local mutexes protect individual native resources, not the entire
session. Several operations on a table are not one transaction unless a
specific primitive provides that atomic contract. Output callbacks may run
while the runtime holds its output mutex, so synchronous re-entry can
deadlock. Forward notifications to the coordinator instead of recursively
asking the same worker to evaluate from inside a callback.
.sh 2 "Define failure publication"
.pp
An evaluation can print and mutate resources before producing a diagnostic.
The interpreter clears bookkeeping such as depth and observable stack state,
but does not undo earlier external effects. A host can buffer output and
publish it only on success, yet that policy cannot undo a native operation
that already modified an external system. Place irreversible actions behind
explicit application-level protocols when atomic publication is required.
.pp
For repeatable jobs, separate planning from execution. Let a script construct
a declarative plan using immutable data, validate the plan in the host, and
then execute it through a controlled interface. This makes retries and
auditing easier because the program's proposed work is inspectable before
effects occur. It also provides a stable boundary for testing without
performing the real external action on every test run.
.sh 2 "Package the runtime as a coherent unit"
.pp
Ship interpreter headers or binaries with a known compatible library tree.
Use explicit paths in deployed applications rather than relying on the
working directory. Preserve relative native include and D module layouts.
The portable host bundle excludes separately opted-in platform services;
inspect installers and build options before promising a service across both
dialects. An existing filename alone does not establish implementation,
dependency availability, or parity.
.pp
Test from an installed prefix as well as from the source checkout. This
catches missing library data, accidental relative includes, and executable
path assumptions. A useful smoke test creates a fresh session, loads the
installed prelude, exercises one permitted operation, rejects one denied
operation, and successfully evaluates another expression afterwards. Those
observations cover packaging, capability enforcement, and recovery together.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read the primitive permission checks, Session limits, and module installers
in both dialects. Compare the CLI's intentionally permissive setup with
a narrow embedded client. Design two deployment profiles: a trusted local
workbench and an isolated batch worker. State their ownership model, library
path, output policy, resource budgets, and retry behavior. Explain which
guarantees come from MetaML and which require the host or operating system.
